WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Image Fight!

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.580
Victory will come only to the worthy!

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.960
Are you?

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:25.000
Nostalgia! And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.140
A chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.860
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.780
And I'm Joe. Can I just read the plot for the game before we even talk about it?

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:48.320
Please. On a fateful day in 20XX, the Earth's moon exploded into four large fragments and a multitude of meteors.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.420
Aliens from afar had succeeded in destroying the West's moon base.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:59.500
One after another, mankind's other military industrial space complexes were being lost.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.240
What mankind dreaded had come to pass.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.360
Scores of unidentified fighters were in the area.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:13.240
In addition, the moon's main computer, still intact after the explosion, had a strange vegetation coiled around it.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:22.300
Their trademark evil exploits being a dead giveaway, invaders from the Boondoggle galaxy had arrived to take over the Earth.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:29.900
To counter these evil forces, leading scientists from all over the globe created the OF-1 fighter ship.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.560
Combat pilots.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:38.040
Oh no. Combat pilots depart the Earth to fend off the invaders and earn everlasting glory.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:46.420
Now, I read all that because that is, for a shmup, that is a pretty detailed plot of what's going on.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.600
Like, it's not just four large fragments of the moon exploded.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.240
There's also a multitude of meteors.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.300
And it's not like just aliens.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.020
These aliens are from the Boondoggle galaxy.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.000
And, you know, there's just like a bunch of fill-in information, even the fighter ship.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:12.860
But, so, why would the game then start with like, an incredibly lengthy simulation experience before you ever get to the actual fights?

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.200
That was pretty crazy.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.300
Now, I have two thoughts here.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:29.880
The first is, this whole story is just another example in a long line of them of like, they do, like they literally do not care about the story.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.080
This was just made up on the spot, probably by the guy that, like, no, because it is in the game too at the end.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.620
But, it just made up on the spot just to, for it to be an excuse to play the game.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:47.020
And, this one actually has proof in calling the galaxy the Boondoggle galaxy.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:51.760
Like, that is literally just a word that means, it's nonsense.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.560
The word is nonsense.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:54.780
It means nonsense.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.640
So, they don't even, they're not even trying to hide it anymore.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.840
So, that's, that's just an annoying, annoyance to me.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.640
And, it's something that I keep seeing in these old NES games.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:04.920
And, it just annoys me.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.260
Now, okay.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.300
Mike, what you were saying.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.080
Wait, so it did say the moon exploded from a multitude of meteors, right?

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.040
No, no, no. Not into.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.140
No, no. It exploded into.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.780
Yeah, so it's a bunch of like fragments.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:18.940
So, why did it explode?

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.020
Well, you know, the computer at the center of the moon, you know.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.260
It's just, it's a, it's a mad lib.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.460
It's a mad lib of things that are vaguely sci-fi.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.860
I don't think I even heard the rest of the story because I spent the whole time you were talking,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.820
trying to figure out, wait, why did the moon explode?

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.680
Did I miss that? No, you know what the funny part is?

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.640
So, Sean, Sean is also hearing like bits and pieces of the story.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.440
And, maybe I didn't do a great job with, you know, like reading it.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.520
But, the moon's main computer is still intact after the explosion.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.000
It's not what caused the explosion.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.240
No, I heard the whole thing.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.500
Okay. But, that's the thing.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.760
It doesn't matter. Yeah, no, it doesn't matter.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:56.840
And, to Joe's point, no, they didn't.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.980
They just say aliens succeeded in exploding the moon.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.160
But, thank God, there was still some vegetation around to keep the main, I know.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.140
What is the goal of destroying the moon?

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.480
Like, aren't you screwing up like the planet that you're probably trying to take over as well by blowing up the moon?

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.400
Probably. It was definitely a surprise to me in the game.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.820
And, maybe it's meant to be.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.960
Yeah. Like a twist, you know?

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.660
Like, oh, this whole time, you know, you were just training.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.100
And, now it's like, this isn't a drill anymore.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.540
Like, they're here. Right?

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:33.420
This is no drill.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.340
Yeah, and that's supposed to have like some weight.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:37.800
Right? Like, oh my God.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:38.740
Like, now it's real.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.700
But, then it doesn't feel any different than the simulation.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:56.900
Simulation. Which is like, kudos to the simulation team for, you know, really like, you know, they really, in that universe, like they really, 20XX, they really got it down pat to what real life feels like.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.540
Because the simulation and the real life levels might as well be the same damn thing.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:03.580
Exactly. Yeah.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.820
And I feel like the simulation part is like longer than the rest of the game.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.080
It is. It is three to two, right?

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.840
Or maybe even longer than that.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.660
I think it's three sim levels, two real levels.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:15.900
Yeah. So, what'd you think?

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.280
Yeah. I was just, I was gonna get into it.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.340
Image Fight is Irem.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.820
Irem developed the game.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.000
They are also the developers of R-Type.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.400
R-Type is Irem's like equivalent to Gradius.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.060
I'm sure you guys have heard of R-Type, right?

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.480
We played it, didn't we?

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:31.640
On the NES?

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.220
Well, maybe I'm thinking of something with Z instead.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.680
Yeah, we didn't play R-Type.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:36.940
Section Z.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:40.140
Yeah. Sorry. It was a letter.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:44.580
You did throw me in for a loop there though Sean, because my next fact was this game came

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.720
out a year, one year after R-Type, but we haven't seen R-Type on the NES.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.220
So that would have been a little embarrassing had we have played R-Type on the NES, but we

00:05:54.220 --> 00:06:01.000
have not. And so I think that's, yeah, that's kind of interesting that Image Fight is like Irem says, put this

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.600
forward instead of their like main franchise.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:11.800
And so it borrows a lot of the qualities from R-Type, but since we haven't played R-Type,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.700
you know, what are those things?

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:22.520
Well, the biggest thing is that the change here from every other shmup is that there are

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:27.360
these pods that you collect and you can have three of them.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.380
There are two different colors of those pods.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:31.800
They are red and blue.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.960
The red one changes directions based on the movement of the ship.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:42.180
So you have like kind of this mini pod ship next to your ship that first appears on the

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.580
left, then the right, and then finally one behind you.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:51.340
And so if you move to the left, it now starts shooting in the left direction as well.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.440
The blue pod doesn't do that.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.900
It always points forward.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:02.340
And I think that's the only difference between these two pods.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:03.520
That is the only difference.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:10.100
But I do think that it's like a, it is a fundamental difference that actually adds something new to

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.980
this formula. Um, one little correction.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.500
I think it's if you move to the left, it's going to shoot to the right.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.040
Oh, okay. And vice versa.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.820
Um, so I think it would be, it would feel a little off if it was just like shooting in

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:24.700
the direction you're moving.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.040
It did feel weird at first, uh, for it to be sort of inverted like that.

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:31.720
But as it went on, it made more sense to me.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.640
Um, but no, I think it's cool.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.680
Like I, I remember, uh, you know, going as far back as like commando or games that, you

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:49.040
are sort of like, like shmups, but not shmups, you know, um, where I was annoyed that you

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:51.240
could only fire in the direction that you're moving.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:57.100
And that made just maneuvering very awkward and frustrating.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:05.000
Um, and I may have mentioned it on an episode or I may have just thought it in my brain or

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:11.160
I may not have thought it at all, but like just being able to do both is really, is really

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.280
nice. Yeah.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:19.140
Because there actually is strategy between like, you wouldn't just take all the red pods,

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.320
right? Even though it seems obvious that like, Oh, well they changed based on the direction.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:29.640
So, uh, you know, like it is kind of a good versatile, uh, strategy to have like a blue

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.700
pod that just always shoots forward.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:35.360
So this way you have additional fire power directly in front of you.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.880
And then maybe like if your red pods are on the left and right, those can kind of take care

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.140
of the enemies on the side.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:48.720
And in general, just like having something about the context of these, if they feel less

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.140
like power ups and they feel more like, Oh, I'm actually building onto my ship.

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:57.020
I think because you can actually kind of see them building onto your ship in that way.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:01.360
But it, so it does feel like you're kind of customizing your ship in that way.

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:04.280
Like with, with the different combinations of the blues and the red.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:10.940
Yeah. It's kind of like a load out, um, as opposed to just like a straight power up.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:19.400
Um, cause yeah, they are, uh, additive and like, so your, your main ship will always shoot

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.280
just forward like a regular shmup.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:26.120
So you could just do all reds and just sort of orient yourself.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:30.240
Um, if, if you really want to focus all your fire forward.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.420
So there's like a lot of flexibility in how you play.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:40.300
And then there's also upgrades for like your main hull where you can, uh, have different,

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.980
uh, projectiles or just, I think there's one that just makes it a shield.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.260
Um, so there's a lot of, there's a lot of customization in here.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:55.120
And the way it works since you can only have three at a time is that, uh, whatever you,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:00.480
whatever, whenever you pick up the next pod, it overwrites, um, the previous pod.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.720
So, uh, you know, the, the, it does kind of become a game that if you are liking your load

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:12.220
out of, like I suggested two reds on the side and a blue on your back, you now have to dodge

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:17.060
all other pods in the game to make sure that you don't, uh, bust that load out.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.640
Not that that's incredibly challenging.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.500
Remember the whole game is dodging.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:28.500
So, um, it's not the biggest deal, but definitely, um, you know, a strange choice when you consider

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.520
that the other power up in this game, the, uh, I think they're described as components,

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:41.000
uh, in the manual are things that cannot, uh, like be removed from the ship or interchanged

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:48.640
with the other ones. Once you collect one of these components, which change how the main ship, the, uh, OF1, how,

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:52.380
how that shoots, it changes the direction, the rate of fire.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:58.920
Now you have, um, now you have to like get hit in order for that to come off.

00:10:59.080 --> 00:10:59.920
Yeah. That's what I was going to say.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:05.880
Like maybe it's kind of counter to how they expected you to play this game, but I just would

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:10.700
go and take chip damage against a wall, not chip damage.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:16.140
It would be, I'll go hit a wall and then go back to the upgrade to change what I wanted

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:22.500
it to be. So there's still, there's still some room for, for error there.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:29.440
Yeah. But I, I actually compared to some other ways we've seen this done, prefer this because

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:33.620
other times it's like, anytime you run into a powerup, it just switches out the powerup

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:40.460
that you have. And while this is obviously far from the ideal way to do it, uh, it's at least.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:47.060
Like it's your choice to decide like, okay, I want to, I want to swap this out.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.000
So I'm going to lose this.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:51.040
Like it's a weird way that you have to like take deliberate damage to do it.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.460
But at least it's not like I never, I felt like, Oh, like I accidentally ran into this

00:11:55.460 --> 00:12:00.400
powerup and now I lost the powerup I wanted because I, you know, I'm being punished for

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:07.620
getting a powerup. That is still a, like a thing you have to be cognizant of with the pods at least as,

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:12.200
as Mike was describing, but I appreciate that it wasn't for everything.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:17.880
And then another change from other shmups is that the ship has four different levels of

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.680
speed that you can switch between.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:26.120
And the way to do this is actually by just pressing the A button on your controller and

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:32.340
you adjust the speed from one, two, three, four, then back down to three, two, one.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:37.220
This way you're not going from one, two, three, four back all the way down to one.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:41.560
You're kind of, uh, you know, able to fine tune it a little bit as you slow down.

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:47.840
This is a big change from, uh, the other means of speed that we've seen in games like

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:55.000
Gradius, where you have to acquire the powerup and select to permanently boost your speed.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:00.440
Um, something that is good at a, at a certain point and then kind of becomes detrimental,

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:05.980
uh, to a shmup is, you know, being too fast and colliding with things that you didn't mean

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:11.120
to just because, uh, just like adjusting your, the sensitivity of your mouse cursor.

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:15.940
On a computer can swing you over from, you know, like one window to the other too quickly.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:17.540
That's what happens in a shmup.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.500
You wind up being able to move too fast in this one.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.560
Being able to control your speeds is a really neat thing.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:30.100
Was it worth like removing a whole button, uh, to, to do that?

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:33.880
Sure. Because I never felt like it should be doing anything else.

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:36.700
Well, I mean, it does several things, right?

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:43.660
Like it, you can then press that button, the same button that you use to change gears, which

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:48.980
I also didn't really notice that big of a difference in the speed, but maybe that's just a me problem.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:56.800
Um, but you also sort of do a, um, like a, it like kicks the back burner on.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:00.840
I think you can use that as sort of a short range.

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:02.360
Um, yes, that is true.

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:07.840
Yeah. It can, it can, it can take off enemies on, on that regard too, of like, um, the, the heat

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.280
from your jets.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:13.520
Which is helpful, especially when you don't have any power ups, so you can't shoot behind

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:18.020
yourself. Um, and also I don't know the rhyme or reason to it.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:20.560
Again, maybe I should have read the manual for this.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:27.500
Um, sometimes it'll launch your pods forward and just sort of take out whatever's in front

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.260
of you. Oh, that's A and B.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:30.640
Oh, okay.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.880
Yeah. If you press them both at the same time, that's your special.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:40.080
Okay, cool. Going back to the, um, what you said about the, the kind of engine, you can like, you

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.520
can shoot it out behind you.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:43.760
Did anyone, did you guys find that useful ever?

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.840
Like, I thought that was cool, but like, did you ever use it in like an actual practical

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:49.440
situation? Or was it just behind you?

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.680
No, because by then I had the red pod, right?

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:53.860
Like I was just using that to shoot from behind.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:58.960
But at least it's there. And like when I didn't have it, I was just like, you got to get really close in order

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:02.320
to do it. Like at that point, it's going to fly away if it's behind me.

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:07.060
I agree. I was wrong on the number of simulation stages.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:08.040
It's five.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:12.180
I was going to point it out, but it seemed petty.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:18.100
Now, what's crazy about that is that five simulation stages, uh, does feel like a lot,

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:24.320
but did you know that there's actually a way to like fail those simulation stages and have

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:28.680
to play an additional stage to get to the, to the real world?

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.980
Because I'm, I'm me.

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:32.100
That was me.

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:38.860
So Sean, what happens if you don't get above an average kill rate of 90%?

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:47.060
Uh, you have to do like, it's called a penalty round and it just forces you to do another

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:53.260
level, which is weird that they're making you play, that they're punishing you by giving

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:54.420
you more of the game.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:58.600
Um, and I guess it's like, it's relatively tough.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.160
Like the game is tough.

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:04.240
Uh, but it was, it's odd.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.620
It was on a black background.

00:16:06.140 --> 00:16:11.920
It was just sort of like the same three enemies for longer than I would have expected.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:14.700
Um, but yeah, that's what it does to you.

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:20.820
And that's kind of funny because if, you know, if the twist of the game is that this whole thing

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:24.860
was simulation and I keep saying twist, but they're, they're spelling it out for you that

